{
  "A0100": "A0100 maintains a nearly level, high-altitude eastward run while steadily increasing speed, rather than joining A0200's descending turn. This appears most plausibly intended to preserve energy and create a separated northern approach or supporting position against the southbound B formation; the aircraft remains alive and retains its recorded missile count, with no observed transition requiring a more aggressive commitment.",
  "A0200": "A0200 commits to a sustained descending turn toward the opposing formation, driving eastward across the engagement while trading altitude for turning geometry and speed. The maneuver is most consistent with an aggressive attack approach or close-pass repositioning against the converging B aircraft; the simultaneous removal of both recorded A missiles and B0200's subsequent inactivity marks an exchange, but does not establish a launch, target, or hit. A0200 then continues descending and turning after that exchange.",
  "B0100": "B0100 initially presses southward toward the A formation, but during the middle of the window transitions into a pronounced bank that changes its motion toward the southeast while continuing to lose altitude. This appears most plausibly to be a defensive break or lateral repositioning as the engagement passes through close geometry, potentially after B0200 becomes inactive; the aircraft survives and continues maneuvering as A0200 descends away, although the data do not establish whether a missile specifically triggered the turn.",
  "B0200": "B0200 becomes inactive and loses valid flight data at approximately 27.2 seconds, so no tactical intention can be inferred for its behavior during the remainder of the window."
}

